1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,883
The nose of a Boeing seven forty-seven

2
00:00:02,883 --> 00:00:05,538
freighter can swing up like a giant

3
00:00:05,538 --> 00:00:08,193
mouth, and a forty foot long pallet

4
00:00:08,193 --> 00:00:09,787
can roll straight in.

5
00:00:09,967 --> 00:00:12,762
That trick is the reason the pilots

6
00:00:12,762 --> 00:00:14,919
of every seven forty-seven,

7
00:00:14,919 --> 00:00:16,836
even the passenger ones,

8
00:00:16,836 --> 00:00:18,992
sit on top of the airplane,

9
00:00:18,992 --> 00:00:20,590
in that famous hump.

10
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:23,833
Cargo planes are full of parts like

11
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:26,459
that, doors in strange places,

12
00:00:26,459 --> 00:00:27,947
floors that roll,

13
00:00:27,947 --> 00:00:29,872
tails that swing open.

14
00:00:30,052 --> 00:00:33,117
Today, ten parts of cargo planes,

15
00:00:33,117 --> 00:00:35,346
and why each one exists.

16
00:00:35,697 --> 00:00:39,864
A passenger airplane is built around people.

17
00:00:40,044 --> 00:00:43,993
A cargo airplane is built around boxes,

18
00:00:43,993 --> 00:00:48,346
engines, helicopters, even other airplanes.

19
00:00:48,526 --> 00:00:51,135
Everything has to get in and out

20
00:00:51,135 --> 00:00:54,151
fast, stay perfectly still in flight,

21
00:00:54,151 --> 00:00:55,863
and never catch fire.

22
00:00:56,043 --> 00:00:57,867
That changes the doors,

23
00:00:57,867 --> 00:00:59,532
the floor, the walls,

24
00:00:59,532 --> 00:01:02,386
and sometimes the whole shape of the

25
00:01:02,386 --> 00:01:03,021
machine.

26
00:01:03,371 --> 00:01:06,552
As always, every fact here comes from

27
00:01:06,552 --> 00:01:07,842
a named source,

28
00:01:07,842 --> 00:01:12,656
manufacturers, accident reports and aviation references,

29
00:01:12,656 --> 00:01:15,236
all listed in the description.

30
00:01:15,416 --> 00:01:17,134
Let's start with the nose.

31
00:01:17,485 --> 00:01:19,447
Why does the nose of a seven

32
00:01:19,447 --> 00:01:21,339
forty-seven freighter open?

33
00:01:21,519 --> 00:01:23,999
So long cargo can go straight in,

34
00:01:23,999 --> 00:01:25,803
with nothing in the way.

35
00:01:25,983 --> 00:01:28,336
In the early nineteen sixties,

36
00:01:28,336 --> 00:01:31,866
Boeing designed a huge military transport for

37
00:01:31,866 --> 00:01:33,983
a US Air Force competition,

38
00:01:33,983 --> 00:01:36,101
and it needed a door at the

39
00:01:36,101 --> 00:01:36,571
front.

40
00:01:36,751 --> 00:01:38,415
To make room for it,

41
00:01:38,415 --> 00:01:40,494
the cockpit was moved up,

42
00:01:40,494 --> 00:01:42,324
above the cargo floor.

43
00:01:42,504 --> 00:01:44,820
Boeing lost that competition,

44
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:47,455
but the idea moved into the seven

45
00:01:47,455 --> 00:01:48,414
forty-seven.

46
00:01:48,764 --> 00:01:49,660
At the time,

47
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:53,395
many engineers believed supersonic jets would soon

48
00:01:53,395 --> 00:01:55,038
carry most passengers,

49
00:01:55,038 --> 00:01:57,951
and the seven forty-seven would end its

50
00:01:57,951 --> 00:01:59,445
life as a freighter.

51
00:01:59,625 --> 00:02:02,081
So Boeing kept the cockpit up high

52
00:02:02,081 --> 00:02:04,248
and made the nose able to open

53
00:02:04,248 --> 00:02:06,126
on the freighter versions.

54
00:02:06,306 --> 00:02:08,778
Supersonic jets never took over,

55
00:02:08,778 --> 00:02:11,481
but the hump became the most famous

56
00:02:11,481 --> 00:02:12,872
shape in aviation,

57
00:02:12,872 --> 00:02:15,343
and the nose door made the seven

58
00:02:15,343 --> 00:02:17,429
forty-seven a cargo legend.

59
00:02:17,779 --> 00:02:21,148
On the seven forty-seven four hundred freighter,

60
00:02:21,148 --> 00:02:23,815
pallets or containers up to forty feet

61
00:02:23,815 --> 00:02:26,552
long can be loaded straight through the

62
00:02:26,552 --> 00:02:28,587
nose on motor-driven rollers.

63
00:02:28,767 --> 00:02:31,496
It can carry around one hundred twenty

64
00:02:31,496 --> 00:02:32,645
tonnes of cargo.

65
00:02:32,825 --> 00:02:35,852
Things that would never turn the corner

66
00:02:35,852 --> 00:02:37,016
of a side door,

67
00:02:37,016 --> 00:02:40,042
like oil drilling equipment or parts of

68
00:02:40,042 --> 00:02:43,146
other aircraft, simply roll straight in.

69
00:02:43,497 --> 00:02:45,764
Why do cargo planes also have a

70
00:02:45,764 --> 00:02:47,446
giant door on the side,

71
00:02:47,446 --> 00:02:48,617
behind the wing?

72
00:02:48,797 --> 00:02:51,669
Because not every airport has the special

73
00:02:51,669 --> 00:02:53,631
loaders needed for the nose,

74
00:02:53,631 --> 00:02:56,013
and not every freighter has a nose

75
00:02:56,013 --> 00:02:56,784
that opens.

76
00:02:56,964 --> 00:02:59,141
A large side cargo door lets a

77
00:02:59,141 --> 00:03:01,898
standard loader lift pallets up to the

78
00:03:01,898 --> 00:03:03,640
main deck from the side.

79
00:03:03,820 --> 00:03:07,271
Many freighters converted from passenger jets get

80
00:03:07,271 --> 00:03:09,595
one of these doors cut into their

81
00:03:09,595 --> 00:03:10,229
fuselage.

82
00:03:10,579 --> 00:03:13,290
Cutting a giant hole in the side

83
00:03:13,290 --> 00:03:16,509
of a pressurized fuselage is a serious

84
00:03:16,509 --> 00:03:16,848
job.

85
00:03:17,028 --> 00:03:19,092
The edges of the hole must be

86
00:03:19,092 --> 00:03:21,725
reinforced with new frames and beams,

87
00:03:21,725 --> 00:03:24,856
because the fuselage is basically a pressure

88
00:03:24,856 --> 00:03:27,347
vessel, and the forces that the old

89
00:03:27,347 --> 00:03:29,838
skin carried must now go around the

90
00:03:29,838 --> 00:03:30,194
door.

91
00:03:30,374 --> 00:03:32,843
The door itself has heavy latches,

92
00:03:32,843 --> 00:03:35,167
and the crew gets warnings if it

93
00:03:35,167 --> 00:03:36,620
is not fully locked.

94
00:03:36,970 --> 00:03:38,520
Below the main deck,

95
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:41,312
there are smaller doors to the belly

96
00:03:41,312 --> 00:03:44,723
holds, for containers up to about sixty-four

97
00:03:44,723 --> 00:03:45,654
inches high.

98
00:03:45,834 --> 00:03:48,165
That is the same space where your

99
00:03:48,165 --> 00:03:50,919
suitcase travels on a passenger flight.

100
00:03:51,099 --> 00:03:53,654
In fact, much of the world's air

101
00:03:53,654 --> 00:03:56,769
cargo flies in the bellies of passenger

102
00:03:56,769 --> 00:04:00,282
airplanes, right under the passengers' feet.

103
00:04:00,633 --> 00:04:02,827
Why is the floor of a cargo

104
00:04:02,827 --> 00:04:05,671
plane covered in rollers and balls?

105
00:04:05,851 --> 00:04:09,072
Because cargo comes in standard pallets and

106
00:04:09,072 --> 00:04:12,518
containers that can weigh several tonnes each,

107
00:04:12,518 --> 00:04:14,466
and nobody can carry them.

108
00:04:14,646 --> 00:04:15,756
Near the door,

109
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:18,134
ball mats let a pallet turn in

110
00:04:18,134 --> 00:04:19,243
any direction.

111
00:04:19,423 --> 00:04:22,057
Along the length of the airplane,

112
00:04:22,057 --> 00:04:25,569
rows of rollers and motor-driven wheels push

113
00:04:25,569 --> 00:04:27,085
it toward the back,

114
00:04:27,085 --> 00:04:28,920
one position at a time.

115
00:04:29,271 --> 00:04:32,238
A loadmaster can move a pallet weighing

116
00:04:32,238 --> 00:04:35,052
several tonnes with a small joystick.

117
00:04:35,232 --> 00:04:38,007
And the containers themselves are clever.

118
00:04:38,187 --> 00:04:40,504
Many of them are shaped with a

119
00:04:40,504 --> 00:04:43,129
sloped side, so they fit the curve

120
00:04:43,129 --> 00:04:45,137
of the fuselage perfectly,

121
00:04:45,137 --> 00:04:47,916
wasting as little space as possible.

122
00:04:48,096 --> 00:04:50,468
They are called unit load devices,

123
00:04:50,468 --> 00:04:52,840
and their shapes are standardized,

124
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:55,072
so the same container can fly on

125
00:04:55,072 --> 00:04:56,467
different airplanes.

126
00:04:56,817 --> 00:04:59,150
Speed is money in cargo.

127
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:01,703
A freighter on the ground is not

128
00:05:01,703 --> 00:05:02,964
earning anything.

129
00:05:03,144 --> 00:05:06,660
With rollers, motors and standard containers,

130
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:09,395
a big freighter can be unloaded and

131
00:05:09,395 --> 00:05:12,130
loaded again in a short turnaround.

132
00:05:12,310 --> 00:05:14,748
The floor is not just a floor.

133
00:05:14,928 --> 00:05:17,622
It is a conveyor belt that flies.

134
00:05:17,972 --> 00:05:20,168
Why is there a strong net or

135
00:05:20,168 --> 00:05:22,835
wall right behind the cockpit of a

136
00:05:22,835 --> 00:05:23,777
cargo plane?

137
00:05:23,957 --> 00:05:25,559
It is the cargo barrier.

138
00:05:25,739 --> 00:05:28,385
If the airplane stops suddenly,

139
00:05:28,385 --> 00:05:31,118
in a crash landing or a rejected

140
00:05:31,118 --> 00:05:34,704
takeoff, tonnes of cargo would keep moving

141
00:05:34,704 --> 00:05:35,387
forward.

142
00:05:35,567 --> 00:05:37,924
The barrier is there to stop it

143
00:05:37,924 --> 00:05:40,129
before it reaches the pilots.

144
00:05:40,309 --> 00:05:44,129
Aviation rules require items inside an airplane

145
00:05:44,129 --> 00:05:47,542
to be restrained against very high forces,

146
00:05:47,542 --> 00:05:50,224
up to nine times their own weight

147
00:05:50,224 --> 00:05:50,874
forward.

148
00:05:51,225 --> 00:05:54,490
The cargo itself is also locked to

149
00:05:54,490 --> 00:05:55,451
the floor.

150
00:05:55,631 --> 00:05:58,689
Pallets are held by locks built into

151
00:05:58,689 --> 00:06:00,049
the floor rails,

152
00:06:00,049 --> 00:06:02,598
and the load on each pallet is

153
00:06:02,598 --> 00:06:05,232
tied down with nets and straps.

154
00:06:05,412 --> 00:06:09,404
The loadmaster calculates where every pallet goes,

155
00:06:09,404 --> 00:06:12,519
because the weight must be spread along

156
00:06:12,519 --> 00:06:15,233
the airplane to keep the center of

157
00:06:15,233 --> 00:06:17,150
gravity in a safe range.

158
00:06:17,500 --> 00:06:18,998
When that goes wrong,

159
00:06:18,998 --> 00:06:19,926
it is deadly.

160
00:06:20,106 --> 00:06:22,292
In two thousand thirteen,

161
00:06:22,292 --> 00:06:26,227
a cargo Boeing seven forty-seven crashed just

162
00:06:26,227 --> 00:06:28,500
after takeoff from Bagram,

163
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:29,812
in Afghanistan.

164
00:06:29,992 --> 00:06:33,231
Heavy armored vehicles inside broke free,

165
00:06:33,231 --> 00:06:37,260
slid backward, and damaged the airplane's controls.

166
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:41,721
The investigators blamed improper restraint of the

167
00:06:41,721 --> 00:06:42,235
cargo.

168
00:06:42,415 --> 00:06:43,922
On a cargo plane,

169
00:06:43,922 --> 00:06:46,316
a loose load is not a mess.

170
00:06:46,496 --> 00:06:47,889
It is an emergency.

171
00:06:48,239 --> 00:06:51,897
Why does the Antonov An-124 kneel down

172
00:06:51,897 --> 00:06:53,245
on the ground,

173
00:06:53,245 --> 00:06:54,496
like a camel?

174
00:06:54,676 --> 00:06:57,231
Its landing gear can lower the nose

175
00:06:57,231 --> 00:06:58,398
of the airplane,

176
00:06:58,398 --> 00:07:00,952
so the cargo floor becomes a gentle

177
00:07:00,952 --> 00:07:01,317
ramp.

178
00:07:01,497 --> 00:07:04,651
Then trucks, tanks and heavy machines can

179
00:07:04,651 --> 00:07:07,727
be driven or winched straight in through

180
00:07:07,727 --> 00:07:08,881
the front door.

181
00:07:09,061 --> 00:07:12,616
The An-124 first flew on the twenty-fourth

182
00:07:12,616 --> 00:07:15,324
of December nineteen eighty-two,

183
00:07:15,324 --> 00:07:17,524
and it is still one of the

184
00:07:17,524 --> 00:07:19,893
biggest cargo planes flying.

185
00:07:20,244 --> 00:07:23,671
The An-124 has doors at both ends,

186
00:07:23,671 --> 00:07:26,997
so vehicles can drive in one side

187
00:07:26,997 --> 00:07:28,812
and out the other.

188
00:07:28,992 --> 00:07:31,617
It can carry about one hundred twenty

189
00:07:31,617 --> 00:07:33,817
tonnes in its standard version,

190
00:07:33,817 --> 00:07:35,946
and up to one hundred fifty in

191
00:07:35,946 --> 00:07:37,223
a special version.

192
00:07:37,403 --> 00:07:38,709
Inside the cabin,

193
00:07:38,709 --> 00:07:41,397
there is an overhead crane that can

194
00:07:41,397 --> 00:07:42,857
lift thirty tonnes,

195
00:07:42,857 --> 00:07:45,315
plus a winch that can pull loads

196
00:07:45,315 --> 00:07:48,003
of up to one hundred twenty tonnes.

197
00:07:48,183 --> 00:07:50,150
It does not need much help from

198
00:07:50,150 --> 00:07:50,911
the airport.

199
00:07:51,262 --> 00:07:54,793
The only airplane that could carry more

200
00:07:54,793 --> 00:07:56,875
was its bigger brother,

201
00:07:56,875 --> 00:07:58,867
the six engine An-225,

202
00:07:58,867 --> 00:08:02,217
built to carry the Soviet Buran space

203
00:08:02,217 --> 00:08:02,941
shuttle.

204
00:08:03,121 --> 00:08:05,651
It was destroyed at Hostomel,

205
00:08:05,651 --> 00:08:09,054
in Ukraine, in two thousand twenty-two.

206
00:08:09,234 --> 00:08:12,046
Today, when a huge turbine,

207
00:08:12,046 --> 00:08:15,484
a satellite or a power plant part

208
00:08:15,484 --> 00:08:16,838
needs to fly,

209
00:08:16,838 --> 00:08:20,275
the call often goes to an An-124.

210
00:08:20,625 --> 00:08:23,632
Why do military cargo planes open their

211
00:08:23,632 --> 00:08:24,943
tail like a ramp?

212
00:08:25,123 --> 00:08:27,666
Because the cargo often has wheels.

213
00:08:27,846 --> 00:08:29,715
With a ramp at the back,

214
00:08:29,715 --> 00:08:33,063
jeeps, trucks and helicopters can be driven

215
00:08:33,063 --> 00:08:34,777
or pushed straight in,

216
00:08:34,777 --> 00:08:37,424
without special airport equipment.

217
00:08:37,604 --> 00:08:41,177
That is why military transports like the

218
00:08:41,177 --> 00:08:44,214
C-130 Hercules and the C-17 have a

219
00:08:44,214 --> 00:08:46,983
high wing, a tail lifted up out

220
00:08:46,983 --> 00:08:47,966
of the way,

221
00:08:47,966 --> 00:08:50,914
and a fuselage that sits close to

222
00:08:50,914 --> 00:08:51,896
the ground.

223
00:08:52,247 --> 00:08:54,870
The ramp can also open in flight.

224
00:08:55,050 --> 00:08:57,929
That is how paratroopers jump and how

225
00:08:57,929 --> 00:09:00,263
cargo is dropped by parachute.

226
00:09:00,443 --> 00:09:02,689
Pallets slide out of the back,

227
00:09:02,689 --> 00:09:04,785
pulled by a small parachute,

228
00:09:04,785 --> 00:09:06,881
and a big one opens to bring

229
00:09:06,881 --> 00:09:07,630
them down.

230
00:09:07,810 --> 00:09:09,704
In some low level drops,

231
00:09:09,704 --> 00:09:12,939
the airplane flies just above the ground,

232
00:09:12,939 --> 00:09:15,306
and the cargo is pulled out by

233
00:09:15,306 --> 00:09:17,911
parachute and lands on its skids.

234
00:09:18,261 --> 00:09:20,250
All of this changes the shape of

235
00:09:20,250 --> 00:09:21,059
the airplane.

236
00:09:21,239 --> 00:09:22,677
The wing is on top,

237
00:09:22,677 --> 00:09:25,023
so it does not block the cabin.

238
00:09:25,203 --> 00:09:27,995
The landing gear is in bulges on

239
00:09:27,995 --> 00:09:30,962
the sides, so the floor stays low.

240
00:09:31,142 --> 00:09:32,949
And the engines are high,

241
00:09:32,949 --> 00:09:35,335
away from rocks and dust on rough

242
00:09:35,335 --> 00:09:35,914
runways.

243
00:09:36,094 --> 00:09:38,435
Look at any military transport,

244
00:09:38,435 --> 00:09:40,852
and you are looking at a machine

245
00:09:40,852 --> 00:09:43,118
designed around its back door.

246
00:09:43,468 --> 00:09:45,863
Why does the tail of this strange

247
00:09:45,863 --> 00:09:48,622
seven forty-seven swing open sideways?

248
00:09:48,802 --> 00:09:50,721
This is the Dreamlifter,

249
00:09:50,721 --> 00:09:54,399
a seven forty-seven heavily modified by Boeing

250
00:09:54,399 --> 00:09:56,877
to carry the giant parts of the

251
00:09:56,877 --> 00:09:58,316
seven eight seven,

252
00:09:58,316 --> 00:10:00,954
like wings and fuselage sections,

253
00:10:00,954 --> 00:10:04,232
between factories in different countries.

254
00:10:04,412 --> 00:10:06,848
The parts are too big for any

255
00:10:06,848 --> 00:10:09,704
normal door, so the entire back of

256
00:10:09,704 --> 00:10:12,644
the airplane swings open on hinges.

257
00:10:12,994 --> 00:10:16,471
The Dreamlifter's fuselage was enlarged into a

258
00:10:16,471 --> 00:10:17,908
huge bulging shape,

259
00:10:17,908 --> 00:10:20,478
with far more volume than a normal

260
00:10:20,478 --> 00:10:22,595
seven forty-seven freighter.

261
00:10:22,775 --> 00:10:24,980
Its cargo is big but not very

262
00:10:24,980 --> 00:10:28,174
heavy, so volume matters more than weight.

263
00:10:28,354 --> 00:10:29,848
Only four were built,

264
00:10:29,848 --> 00:10:31,699
and they are a key part of

265
00:10:31,699 --> 00:10:34,190
how the seven eight seven is built,

266
00:10:34,190 --> 00:10:36,966
as the parts come from suppliers spread

267
00:10:36,966 --> 00:10:38,176
around the world.

268
00:10:38,526 --> 00:10:41,320
Opening the tail needs a special vehicle

269
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:42,298
on the ground,

270
00:10:42,298 --> 00:10:44,394
a loader that drives up to the

271
00:10:44,394 --> 00:10:46,420
back and pulls the parts out.

272
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:48,335
It is a big process,

273
00:10:48,335 --> 00:10:52,410
but the alternative was shipping giant fuselage

274
00:10:52,410 --> 00:10:54,838
sections by sea and by road,

275
00:10:54,838 --> 00:10:56,399
which takes weeks.

276
00:10:56,579 --> 00:10:58,233
With the Dreamlifter,

277
00:10:58,233 --> 00:11:00,437
a wing can cross an ocean in

278
00:11:00,437 --> 00:11:00,910
a day.

279
00:11:01,260 --> 00:11:03,827
Why does the Airbus Beluga have that

280
00:11:03,827 --> 00:11:05,253
giant bubble on top?

281
00:11:05,433 --> 00:11:09,034
Because Airbus builds its airliners in pieces

282
00:11:09,034 --> 00:11:11,595
across Europe, wings in Britain,

283
00:11:11,595 --> 00:11:13,835
fuselage sections in France,

284
00:11:13,835 --> 00:11:15,275
Germany and Spain,

285
00:11:15,275 --> 00:11:17,836
and those pieces must fly to the

286
00:11:17,836 --> 00:11:19,516
final assembly lines.

287
00:11:19,696 --> 00:11:22,095
The Beluga's cockpit was lowered,

288
00:11:22,095 --> 00:11:24,275
and a huge cargo bay was built

289
00:11:24,275 --> 00:11:26,819
above it, opening through a door at

290
00:11:26,819 --> 00:11:28,926
the front, above the cockpit.

291
00:11:29,277 --> 00:11:32,053
The newer BelugaXL is based on the

292
00:11:32,053 --> 00:11:34,665
A three thirty and first flew in

293
00:11:34,665 --> 00:11:36,870
July two thousand eighteen.

294
00:11:37,050 --> 00:11:39,120
It can carry two A three fifty

295
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:40,707
wings at the same time.

296
00:11:40,887 --> 00:11:44,364
Its designers even painted a smiling face

297
00:11:44,364 --> 00:11:45,382
on the nose,

298
00:11:45,382 --> 00:11:47,248
with eyes and a mouth,

299
00:11:47,248 --> 00:11:48,944
after a public vote.

300
00:11:49,124 --> 00:11:51,278
It is one of the few aircraft

301
00:11:51,278 --> 00:11:53,803
built to look friendly on purpose.

302
00:11:54,153 --> 00:11:56,998
The Beluga and the Dreamlifter solve the

303
00:11:56,998 --> 00:11:59,203
same problem in different ways.

304
00:11:59,383 --> 00:12:03,297
Both turn big airliners into flying warehouses

305
00:12:03,297 --> 00:12:05,339
for their own factories.

306
00:12:05,519 --> 00:12:09,140
Without them, building a modern airliner would

307
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:11,973
mean trucks, ships and many weeks of

308
00:12:11,973 --> 00:12:14,807
transport for every single airplane.

309
00:12:15,157 --> 00:12:17,248
Why are the walls of a cargo

310
00:12:17,248 --> 00:12:20,683
hold covered in special fire-resistant panels?

311
00:12:20,863 --> 00:12:22,922
They are called cargo liners,

312
00:12:22,922 --> 00:12:24,768
and their job is to hold a

313
00:12:24,768 --> 00:12:27,963
fire inside the cargo compartment long enough

314
00:12:27,963 --> 00:12:29,454
for the crew to land.

315
00:12:29,634 --> 00:12:32,240
Behind those walls run the cables and

316
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:34,917
control systems that fly the airplane.

317
00:12:35,097 --> 00:12:37,037
If a fire burns through,

318
00:12:37,037 --> 00:12:39,300
the pilots can lose control.

319
00:12:39,650 --> 00:12:42,339
That is what happened to UPS flight

320
00:12:42,339 --> 00:12:44,874
six on the third of September two

321
00:12:44,874 --> 00:12:45,873
thousand ten.

322
00:12:46,053 --> 00:12:48,498
Shortly after takeoff from Dubai,

323
00:12:48,498 --> 00:12:51,981
a pallet carrying more than eighty-one thousand

324
00:12:51,981 --> 00:12:54,946
lithium batteries caught fire by itself.

325
00:12:55,126 --> 00:12:57,681
The fire burned through the liner,

326
00:12:57,681 --> 00:12:59,785
damaged the flight controls,

327
00:12:59,785 --> 00:13:01,964
and smoke filled the cockpit.

328
00:13:02,144 --> 00:13:03,443
Both pilots died,

329
00:13:03,443 --> 00:13:07,036
about twenty-seven minutes after the first fire

330
00:13:07,036 --> 00:13:07,647
warning.

331
00:13:07,998 --> 00:13:09,537
After that crash,

332
00:13:09,537 --> 00:13:11,167
the rules changed.

333
00:13:11,347 --> 00:13:15,351
Bulk lithium battery shipments on passenger planes

334
00:13:15,351 --> 00:13:19,435
were restricted, Boeing changed its fire checklists

335
00:13:19,435 --> 00:13:22,077
to keep air flowing to the flight

336
00:13:22,077 --> 00:13:25,921
deck, and the investigators called for automatic

337
00:13:25,921 --> 00:13:28,483
fire suppression in cargo holds.

338
00:13:28,663 --> 00:13:32,891
Today, cargo airlines use fire containment covers

339
00:13:32,891 --> 00:13:34,875
and special containers.

340
00:13:35,055 --> 00:13:37,552
Every new safety part was paid for

341
00:13:37,552 --> 00:13:38,654
by that flight.

342
00:13:39,004 --> 00:13:40,963
Why is there a seat and a

343
00:13:40,963 --> 00:13:43,941
control panel inside the cargo hold of

344
00:13:43,941 --> 00:13:45,586
a military transport?

345
00:13:45,766 --> 00:13:48,030
It is the loadmaster's station.

346
00:13:48,210 --> 00:13:51,392
The loadmaster is the crew member who

347
00:13:51,392 --> 00:13:54,058
plans where all the cargo goes,

348
00:13:54,058 --> 00:13:57,411
checks that every pallet and vehicle is

349
00:13:57,411 --> 00:14:00,077
locked down, operates the ramp,

350
00:14:00,077 --> 00:14:03,086
and in flight watches the cargo and

351
00:14:03,086 --> 00:14:04,290
runs airdrops.

352
00:14:04,470 --> 00:14:05,848
On a cargo plane,

353
00:14:05,848 --> 00:14:08,603
the cargo has its own crew member.

354
00:14:08,954 --> 00:14:12,720
The loadmaster's numbers decide whether the airplane

355
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:13,807
can fly at all.

356
00:14:13,987 --> 00:14:16,616
If the weight is too far forward

357
00:14:16,616 --> 00:14:17,930
or too far back,

358
00:14:17,930 --> 00:14:21,628
the airplane can become impossible to control

359
00:14:21,628 --> 00:14:22,532
on takeoff.

360
00:14:22,712 --> 00:14:25,078
That is why every piece of cargo

361
00:14:25,078 --> 00:14:27,815
is weighed and its position recorded,

362
00:14:27,815 --> 00:14:30,699
and why the loadmaster signs off before

363
00:14:30,699 --> 00:14:32,104
the airplane moves.

364
00:14:32,454 --> 00:14:35,321
So cargo planes are not just passenger

365
00:14:35,321 --> 00:14:37,585
planes with the seats removed.

366
00:14:37,765 --> 00:14:39,389
Their doors, floors,

367
00:14:39,389 --> 00:14:42,394
walls, tails and even their crews are

368
00:14:42,394 --> 00:14:44,992
designed around one simple goal,

369
00:14:44,992 --> 00:14:47,185
get anything, of any shape,

370
00:14:47,185 --> 00:14:49,540
from one side of the world to

371
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:51,732
the other, safely and fast.

372
00:14:52,082 --> 00:14:53,440
So here are the ten.

373
00:14:53,620 --> 00:14:55,775
A nose that opens and a hump

374
00:14:55,775 --> 00:14:57,161
that came from it.

375
00:14:57,341 --> 00:14:58,891
A giant side door,

376
00:14:58,891 --> 00:15:00,527
a floor that rolls,

377
00:15:00,527 --> 00:15:03,541
and a net that protects the pilots.

378
00:15:03,721 --> 00:15:05,057
Legs that kneel,

379
00:15:05,057 --> 00:15:06,644
a ramp at the back,

380
00:15:06,644 --> 00:15:08,648
a tail that swings open,

381
00:15:08,648 --> 00:15:10,987
a bubble for airplane parts,

382
00:15:10,987 --> 00:15:13,910
fireproof walls, and a seat for the

383
00:15:13,910 --> 00:15:16,750
person who makes sure it all stays

384
00:15:16,750 --> 00:15:17,502
in place.

385
00:15:17,852 --> 00:15:20,304
Next time you see a cargo plane,

386
00:15:20,304 --> 00:15:21,683
look at the doors.

387
00:15:21,863 --> 00:15:23,683
They tell you exactly what it was

388
00:15:23,683 --> 00:15:24,510
built to carry.

389
